="float"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Type D Glomus jugulare tumor (Patient 5) showing intracranial extension, compressing pons and brainstem.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ceo-2-28-g002"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Angiography showing ascending pharyngeal artery as tumor feeding vessel.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ceo-2-28-g003"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Clinical presentation of 9 cases</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ceo-2-28-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>CN: cranial nerve; APA: ascending pharyngeal artery; OA: occipital artery; PAA: posterior auricular artery; SNHL: sensorineural hearing loss.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Treatment outcome of 9 cases</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ceo-2-28-i002"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>ITFA: infratemporal fossa approach; TR: total resection; PR: partial rection; GKS: gamma knife surgery; SNHL: sensorineural hearing loss; CN: cranial nerve.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>